 know  exactly how rock hard his abs are at all  times and a robotic reincarnation of mr  belvedere and you get a cartoon as  eccentric as it is hilarious  space ghost coast to coast is one of  those once in a generation shows that  combines nostalgia satire and sheer  baffling weirdness it's a talk show  starring one of hannah barbera's lesser  known heroes in which actual real life  celebrities are interviewed it's strange  but space ghost only grew more surreal  with time it's success acting as a sort  of springboard for some of the most  peculiar work adult swim has ever aired  even when compared to strange successors  like tom goes to the mayor that's how  groundbreaking stage setting and  perfectly odd space ghost was and  honestly we wouldn't mind if this iconic  cartoon got a reboot nobody cares moby  nobody cares  no one it's also one of the shows on  this list most easily mistaken by a kid  for something aimed at them space ghost  coast-to-coast doesn't pelt the viewer  with violence or intimate situations the  way other adult-oriented animated shows  do instead it dwells with surreal calm  in a valley of absurd early seasons see  space ghost not quite understanding of  who his guests are or how the world  works even assuming everyone is a  superhero and asking them about their  powers later seasons get even weirder as  we see him hawk a shampoo that makes you  smarter get afflicted by a curse that  drives him to cannibalism and becomes  saddled with terrible licensing deals  the show is a creative force of nature  responsible in large part for what would  go on to become an entire generation of  adult animation  little ones will likely just be creeped  out by its flights of dark fancy or  given an entirely incorrect idea of  space ghost's popularity as a  conventional superhero  generally speaking the west has always  seen cartoons as kids stuff adult  animation has certainly made gains the  simpsons isn't exactly obscure but even  then those gains have mostly been  limited to tv sitcoms not movies  thanks to disney dominance one could be  forgiven for thinking even the few  animated films directed at adults that  we've gotten are part of a very recent  trend that probably goes back no further  than a decade or two that assumption is  off by about 50 years thanks to 1972's  fritz the cat this isn't just an  animated movie that happens to be  intended for adults it was originally  x-rated the first animated film in the  united states to be so and for a whole  lot of very good reasons adapted from  the legendarily taboo work of cartoonist  r crum fritz the cat follows the feline  wannabe poet on a journey through the  free love era's deepest pits of hedonism  though director ralph bakshi has  correctly identified that fritz the cat  is a little less shocking by today's  standards it's still the kind of stuff  that you don't want to see a whole lot  of in mainstream movies animated or  otherwise  this is after all a movie in which a  cartoon cat engages in group intercourse  gets high hides from the cops and ends  up on a motorcycle with a neo-nazi  rabbit in isolation however the  characters often look like the soft  cuddly sort of mascots one might find on  school supplies in his big red sweater  fritz in particular is eye-catchingly  cute and just as wildly horrifically  inappropriate for the crowd most  cartoons about fuzzy animals are meant  for  eonflux contains multitudes gnostic  philosophy late 90s club wear and techno  utopianism are all a big part of the  slickly animated series it's a show with  a vision of the future that's as stylish  as it is horrifying the titular eon is  an assassin locked in eternal war  between the anarchic country of monaca  and the ruthlessly ordered nation of  bregna there's nowhere else to live upon  the blighted earth of the far future  making eon's mission as crucial as it is  pointless her world is one of extremes  emphasized by the show's  boundary-pushing aesthetics eon herself  is so ludicrously sexualized that she  goes entirely round the bend and ends up  grotesque  that purposefully off-putting style has  its roots and animation of a very  different sort the kind for kids far too  young for eon flux creator peter chung  got his start working on rugrats the  standard bearer of the lumpy 1990s  nickelodeon look indeed eon looks  startlingly natural when placed beside  tommy pickles and cartoons like the wild  thornberries that carried on its  aesthetic legacy  from an animation nerd point of view  this is a fascinating bit of connected  tissue between some very unlikely works  of art but for anyone in charge of  making sure kids don't get a spy  thriller laced with bondage imagery with  the playpen bound adventures of a band  of early 90s toddlers it's to be noted  with care  gendy tartakovsky's neon tale of a  time-displaced samurai was one of the  coolest things a kid in 2001 could watch  and yes it could be watched by kids  while robots bleeding oil and ravers  with pacifiers hinted at violent films  and drug-soaked parties respectively  samurai jack only ever alluded to these  inspirations borrowing their outlaw cool  without portraying the actual law  breaking at hand what resulted was a  landmark achievement so beloved that it  was given a chance to end its story  properly 13 years after it went off the  air thus was born samurai jack's fifth  season which aired in 2017. jack's  audience had grown up in the intervening  years and the show honored that by  making a conclusive season for the  adults they were instead of the children  they'd been this isn't to say samurai  jack changed its tone entirely it's  pretty easy to imagine a well-adjusted  12 year old who loves the first four  seasons being judged mature enough to  view it by his parents but still the  jack revival's tv 14 rating is earned  blood flows thickly assassins are  impaled upon spears and there are brief  implications of sexuality as it stands  now young fans of samurai jack can enjoy  the first four seasons to their hearts  content but an adult is probably going  to need to make the judgment call of  whether or not they're ready for the  final installment check out one of our  newest videos right here plus more  looper videos about your favorite shows  are coming soon subscribe to our youtube  channel and hit the bell so you don't  miss a single one  [Music]

